{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's silence-completion signature has no causal mechanism linking better-than-chance accuracy on checklist-silent items to genuine reliability rather than to a shared latent prior. On items where the checklist is silent, panelists may systematically infer the correct answer because the silent items covary with a background regularity the checklist's authors relied upon but did not codify; the panel then tracks correctness without exercising any competence the checklist did not already encode. The target's own countercondition concedes that systematic guessing could be bias, not competence, but its main claim still treats better-than-chance accuracy as the signature of genuine reliability. Without a mechanism that separates latent-prior exploitation from externalized competence, silence-completion collapses back into a tracking artifact and cannot distinguish genuine reliability gain within a single evaluation.","target":"6d2470758892e4fd7cd03f7289f430937b0ccdf6780a15aabd40642abafa1019"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's divergence-toward-correctness test requires the checklist to err within the evaluation, but it gives no causal mechanism for why a panel that diverges from a correct checklist should count as more reliable rather than less. The main claim says identity with the checklist indicates no genuine reliability beyond compliance, yet the first countercondition admits that if the checklist is perfectly accurate, divergence is unnecessary and identity does not indicate mere compliance. Because the claim is conditioned on checklist error that may not occur within a single evaluation, the proposed evidence is unavailable precisely when the checklist is most reliable; the target therefore cannot, within a single evaluation, distinguish the case where the panel adds genuine competence from the case where it merely complies with an accurate checklist. The mechanism is circular: divergence toward correctness is only detectable where the checklist already fails, so the test measures checklist imperfection rather than panel reliability.","target":"8911abb395f140f6a9d29da15134309dd39a0242baa8ee98d50a0629df974c2d"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 and SRC_002 both fail to supply a causal mechanism, but SRC_003's shuffled-checklist design has a distinct mechanism defect: it treats robustness to shuffling as evidence of reliability independent of the checklist, yet its own counterconditions concede that panelists may ignore an obviously wrong shuffled checklist or that the checklist may function as a memory aid rather than a decision rule. Under either condition, non-degradation under shuffling is produced by panelists discarding the checklist, not by genuine reliability; the target provides no mechanism to distinguish checklist-rejecting noncompliance from competence. The shuffled-checklist manipulation thus confounds two causal pathways to the same observable (no accuracy degradation), so within a single evaluation it cannot isolate genuine reliability from strategic disregard of an implausible checklist.","target":"befdb3d1f1c3a35cd982a34e9d0c3520a0e7031b1610f043db340adbc09d8fc6"}]}